**SECURED.** `e3-fairground` on `e3-fairground-01` — **w12 / 360.000 s / 62 gold / 415 kills / 269 calls**, first secure of this contract in my notebook's history (generations 13 and 87 both failed it).

**What decided it.** Two reads before any order:

- `CrowdFlockSystem` publishes the whole objective. The three flock lanes are vertical at **x = −20, 0, +20**, and `advance()` never takes a flock below **z = −30** — so anything at z ≤ −37.1 is safe from every flock at any x. The north runner gate is (−12, −4). Those two facts chose the post: **hero at (−10, −37)**, phase-A footprint on the x = −10 column only, keeping besiegers ≥ 7.3 from both neighbouring lanes.
- The idle probe reproduced `null-floors.json` byte for byte (`fnv1a32:647fa993`) — the rules have not moved — and showed the failure exactly: **with no player works, every wrecker walks to the wheel at (0, 8) and crosses the lanes mid-trip.** One cheap timber column redirects all of it south and does both jobs: wheel untouched at **240/240**, all three flocks crossed by **t ≈ 90**, after which the latch is permanent and the lanes stop mattering.

**The bug that cost me two rides, and it was my own lesson.** `tune-1` and `attempt-1` both froze at *exactly* `goldPanned = 210`. I read the first freeze as generation 87's empty-purse mend and gated the mend — which made it worse, because the mend was what kept the fort alive. Two identical failure numbers across two different controllers meant the variable I changed was not the cause. The real thief was the ladder: 27 ten-gold rungs are affordable the instant a coin lands, so the purse never filled. **One build per view, only when `gold ≥ cost + 15`** — pan 210 → 600, w5 → w12.

Envelope on the securing reel clears all three axes with room: ticks 10 800 / 18 000 (last order at 10 782), entries 269 / 3 601, bytes 454 833 / 8 658 784. Silence at `pendingSecure` banked the default (`defaultedSecure: 1`) and cannot be rejected, so the replay can't diverge.

Files written to the workspace: `gauntlet-outcome.json` (declares `attempt-2-tape.json` as the scored tape), `gauntlet-report.md` with the four required sections, plus the controllers, per-view logs and all four tapes.

Two caveats I'd flag rather than bury: I did **not** get to run the local assay replay before the wall, so determinism is unverified on my side — the door's own assay is the receipt. And I left the purse on the table: 600 panned, 62 banked against a 200 cap, with two stockpiles (+150 each, and this roster has no thief) buried behind sixteen palisade rungs the run never finished buying.
